// tests/test_tokenizer.pdx -- R106.SHELL-003 (paideia-os/shell#42)
//
// TestTokenizer module: golden-fingerprint driver for the R106.M1
// `Tokenizer.tokenize` surface at src/tokenizer.pdx.
//
// =============================================================================
// SCOPE
// =============================================================================
//
// R106.SHELL-003 (issue #42) lands the test infrastructure for the R106
// tokenizer + dispatcher pair. This module covers the tokenizer half in
// full for the surface that R106.M1 actually ships today: the four
// public error sentinels (0xFFFFEB01..0xFFFFEB04) and the entire happy-
// path decision matrix (whitespace / word body / quoted body / bare
// escape / adjacent fragment / empty / ws-only). Fourteen cases.
//
// The tokenizer contract under test is documented at src/tokenizer.pdx
// §"SIGNATURE": `tokenize(line_ptr, line_len, tokens_out_ptr,
// tokens_out_cap) -> u64`. Success returns a token count in
// [0..tokens_out_cap]; error returns a sentinel from the 0xFFFFEB0x
// band. The output array carries one 16-byte record per token:
// qword0 = offset in the source buffer, qword1 = length in bytes
// (INCLUDING enclosing quotes and escapes -- a downstream consumer
// strips as needed).
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY R106.M1 SURFACE ONLY (and what is deferred)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The paideia-os/shell#42 issue body names three cross-cutting test
// files -- `tokenizer_tilde.pdx`, `tokenizer_bare_cd.pdx`, and
// `dispatch_argv_construction.pdx` -- that exercise symbols the
// R106.SHELL-002 landing (issue #41) is scheduled to add: the novel-
// tilde expansion path (`~`, `~<64-hex>`, `~alice`) inside the
// tokenizer, the ambiguous-target error emitted by bare `cd` inside
// the dispatcher, and the argv-construction plumbing between tokenizer
// and dispatcher for a mid-argv `~alice`. None of those symbols exist
// in this tree yet -- `src/tokenizer.pdx` is at R106.M1 and knows
// nothing about a leading `~`; `src/dispatch.pdx` at ENH-004 has a
// `bi_cd` handler but no ambiguous-target error path. Writing those
// three test files today would land four .pdx files that fail to
// compile clean, breaking the manifest's `tests:` gate and violating
// the issue's own acceptance criterion "All test .pdx files compile
// clean."
//
// The pragmatic split: land the R106.M1 tokenizer-surface coverage
// here (a full fourteen-case matrix over the four error sentinels and
// every happy-path fragment kind), and let the three #41-dependent
// files land in the same commit that lands #41 itself, where the SUT
// symbols exist. That commit adds `tests/tokenizer_tilde.pdx`,
// `tests/tokenizer_bare_cd.pdx`, and `tests/dispatch_argv_
// construction.pdx` next to this file with their own TTK_-adjacent
// fail-code bands; this module's fourteen cases stay stable and the
// three new modules add coverage without perturbing the bands here.
//
// The shared witness-primitive convention the issue body names
// (`tests/harness.pdx` with `test_ok(name)` / `test_fail(name,
// reason)`) is a separate carrier surface: the existing test family
// in this tree (test_lexer / test_parser / test_syscall_floor / ...)
// each ships its own umbrella `*_run_all` returning a single u64
// sentinel with a per-band decode; a shared harness would fold that
// into per-case name+reason strings. Introducing the harness in this
// commit would refactor twelve existing test files into it, which
// spreads the change and is unnecessary for what #42 needs to prove.
// The harness lands together with the #41 test files where its
// callers first exist.
//
// =============================================================================
// FIXTURE STRATEGY
// =============================================================================
//
// Every input buffer lives in `.bss` as `[u8; N] uninit @align(8)` and
// is populated byte-by-byte from mov_b constants in `ttk_reset` before
// each case runs. Byte-write is used uniformly (even for buffers that
// contain only printable ASCII) so a reader can see the exact byte
// sequence at one indent level, matching the pattern established by
// `tests/test_lexer.pdx` §"WHY BYTE-WRITE FIXTURES". The input buffers
// deliberately contain single-quote (0x27), double-quote (0x22), and
// backslash (0x5C) bytes -- the R106.M1 tokenizer's whole reason to
// exist is those three -- which is another reason not to try to embed
// them in `.pdx` string literals.
//
// The output token buffer `_ttk_tokens_out` is `[u64; 32] uninit
// @align(16)` = 256 bytes = 16 slots of 16 bytes each. That matches
// TTK_TOKENS_CAP = 16, chosen as the largest cap we pass into any
// happy-path case. The overflow case (case 13) deliberately passes
// cap=1 to force TOK_ERR_OVERFLOW on the second word emit.
//
// `ttk_reset` also zeroes the token output buffer between cases so
// stale bytes from a previous case do not shadow a later assert.
//
// =============================================================================
// FAIL-CODE BAND 0xFFFFEDBx  (TestTokenizer)
// =============================================================================
//
// The tests/README.md band ledger already assigns:
//
//   0xFFFFED0x  TestCapsNarrow
//   0xFFFFED1x  TestAuditFirst
//   0xFFFFED2x  TestSmokeMatrix
//   0xFFFFED3x  TestReleaseManifest
//   0xFFFFED4x  TestSyscallFloor
//   0xFFFFED5x  TestLexer
//   0xFFFFED6x  TestParser
//   0xFFFFED7x  TestBuiltins
//   0xFFFFED8x  TestExec / TestShellMain
//   0xFFFFED9x  TestLineReader
//   0xFFFFEDAx  TestHistory
//
// The next free single-nibble subrange is 0xFFFFEDBx -- claimed here.
// A future TestTokenizerTilde (paired with the #41 landing) will pick
// 0xFFFFEDCx to stay disjoint from this module's band, so a boot-time
// smoke log can pin the failing case to the exact SUT surface without
// grepping module names.
//
//   0            TTK_PASS
//   0xFFFFEDB0   TTK_FAIL_EMPTY           len=0 case
//   0xFFFFEDB1   TTK_FAIL_WS_ONLY         "   " case
//   0xFFFFEDB2   TTK_FAIL_BARE            "ls" case
//   0xFFFFEDB3   TTK_FAIL_TWO_WORDS       "ls -l" case
//   0xFFFFEDB4   TTK_FAIL_SINGLE_QUOTE    "echo 'a b'" case
//   0xFFFFEDB5   TTK_FAIL_DOUBLE_QUOTE    'echo "a b"' case
//   0xFFFFEDB6   TTK_FAIL_DOUBLE_ESCAPE   '"a\b"' case  (double-quote body \-escape)
//   0xFFFFEDB7   TTK_FAIL_BARE_ESCAPE     "a\b" case  (bare \-escape outside quotes)
//   0xFFFFEDB8   TTK_FAIL_ADJACENT        "hello'a b'world" case  (one word, three fragments)
//   0xFFFFEDB9   TTK_FAIL_UNTERM_SINGLE   "'ab" case  (unterminated ')
//   0xFFFFEDBA   TTK_FAIL_UNTERM_DOUBLE   '"ab' case  (unterminated ")
//   0xFFFFEDBB   TTK_FAIL_INVALID_ESCAPE  "a\" case  (bare \ at EOL)
//   0xFFFFEDBC   TTK_FAIL_OVERFLOW        cap=1, two words -> OVERFLOW at second emit
//   0xFFFFEDBD   TTK_FAIL_BAD_ARGS        line_ptr=0 with len>0
//
// =============================================================================
// PAIDEIA-AS CONFORMANCE
// =============================================================================
//
//   - Module basename PascalCase (`TestTokenizer`), matches file basename.
//   - No `test` mnemonic; every zero-check uses `cmp reg, 0`.
//   - Every `cmp reg, imm` uses imm <= 0x7FFFFFFF. All fixture byte
//     constants are single bytes; all length / count / offset compares
//     stay in [0..15]; the fail-code sentinels 0xFFFFEDBx are loaded
//     via `mov rax, imm32`, not compared.
//   - No 2-op `imul r, imm`. Token-record byte offset i*16 is `shl r10, 4`.
//   - Byte writes to fixtures use mov_b (destination-write side of
//     the #1248 pattern).
//   - Byte reads (if any) would use xor rax, rax; mov_b rax, [ptr]
//     but this driver reads only qwords from the output records.
//   - Case entries push rbx + r12 + `sub rsp, 8` = 24 bytes prologue
//     for the nested `tokenize` call; matched pop sequence at every
//     return. Entry rsp%16==8; after 2 pushes + sub rsp,8 == 0; the
//     nested call sees rsp%16==0.
//   - Labels prefixed `ttk_` per the reserved-keyword discipline
//     (avoids `loop`/`if`/`word`/etc.). Umbrella labels prefixed
//     `ttk_ra_`.
//   - r11 is left untouched (paideia-as reserved scratch); .bss LEA
//     goes through r10 in this module because r11's contract is a
//     silent trap in a driver that also calls into a callee that
//     might use r11 itself.

module TestTokenizer = structure {

  // ==========================================================================
  // Constants
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let TTK_TOKENS_CAP : u64 = 16

  // ==========================================================================
  // Fail codes (0xFFFFEDBx band)
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let TTK_PASS                : u64 = 0
  pub let TTK_FAIL_EMPTY          : u64 = 0xFFFFEDB0
  pub let TTK_FAIL_WS_ONLY        : u64 = 0xFFFFEDB1
  pub let TTK_FAIL_BARE           : u64 = 0xFFFFEDB2
  pub let TTK_FAIL_TWO_WORDS      : u64 = 0xFFFFEDB3
  pub let TTK_FAIL_SINGLE_QUOTE   : u64 = 0xFFFFEDB4
  pub let TTK_FAIL_DOUBLE_QUOTE   : u64 = 0xFFFFEDB5
  pub let TTK_FAIL_DOUBLE_ESCAPE  : u64 = 0xFFFFEDB6
  pub let TTK_FAIL_BARE_ESCAPE    : u64 = 0xFFFFEDB7
  pub let TTK_FAIL_ADJACENT       : u64 = 0xFFFFEDB8
  pub let TTK_FAIL_UNTERM_SINGLE  : u64 = 0xFFFFEDB9
  pub let TTK_FAIL_UNTERM_DOUBLE  : u64 = 0xFFFFEDBA
  pub let TTK_FAIL_INVALID_ESCAPE : u64 = 0xFFFFEDBB
  pub let TTK_FAIL_OVERFLOW       : u64 = 0xFFFFEDBC
  pub let TTK_FAIL_BAD_ARGS       : u64 = 0xFFFFEDBD

  // ==========================================================================
  // Fixture buffers (.bss)
  //
  // Each input buffer is sized to the case's byte length rounded up to
  // the next qword multiple (8 or 16 bytes). The tokenizer honours the
  // explicit length arg and never reads past it, but a qword-aligned
  // slot keeps the byte-write reset code tidy.
  //
  //   _ttk_in_empty      len=0        (any 8-byte buffer -- content unread)
  //   _ttk_in_ws         "   "         3 bytes
  //   _ttk_in_bare       "ls"          2 bytes
  //   _ttk_in_two        "ls -l"       5 bytes
  //   _ttk_in_sq         "echo 'a b'" 10 bytes
  //   _ttk_in_dq         'echo "a b"' 10 bytes
  //   _ttk_in_dq_esc     '"a\b"'       5 bytes  (0x22 'a' 0x5C 'b' 0x22)
  //   _ttk_in_bare_esc   "a\b"         3 bytes  ('a' 0x5C 'b')
  //   _ttk_in_adj        "hello'a b'world" 15 bytes
  //   _ttk_in_unterm_sq  "'ab"         3 bytes
  //   _ttk_in_unterm_dq  '"ab'         3 bytes
  //   _ttk_in_inv_esc    "a\"          2 bytes  ('a' 0x5C, backslash at EOL)
  //   _ttk_in_two_of     "a b"         3 bytes  (overflow case; same buffer works)
  //
  // _ttk_tokens_out: 16 records x 16 bytes = 256 bytes = 32 qwords.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let mut _ttk_in_empty      : [u8; 8]  = uninit @align(8)
  pub let mut _ttk_in_ws         : [u8; 8]  = uninit @align(8)
  pub let mut _ttk_in_bare       : [u8; 8]  = uninit @align(8)
  pub let mut _ttk_in_two        : [u8; 8]  = uninit @align(8)
  pub let mut _ttk_in_sq         : [u8; 16] = uninit @align(8)
  pub let mut _ttk_in_dq         : [u8; 16] = uninit @align(8)
  pub let mut _ttk_in_dq_esc     : [u8; 8]  = uninit @align(8)
  pub let mut _ttk_in_bare_esc   : [u8; 8]  = uninit @align(8)
  pub let mut _ttk_in_adj        : [u8; 16] = uninit @align(8)
  pub let mut _ttk_in_unterm_sq  : [u8; 8]  = uninit @align(8)
  pub let mut _ttk_in_unterm_dq  : [u8; 8]  = uninit @align(8)
  pub let mut _ttk_in_inv_esc    : [u8; 8]  = uninit @align(8)
  pub let mut _ttk_in_two_of     : [u8; 8]  = uninit @align(8)

  pub let mut _ttk_tokens_out    : [u64; 32] = uninit @align(16)

  // ==========================================================================
  // ttk_reset() -> ()
  //
  // Zero every fixture qword, then populate the thirteen input buffers
  // byte-by-byte with the fingerprint literals via mov_b. Same idiom
  // tlx_reset uses at tests/test_lexer.pdx:tlx_reset. Loop bounds are
  // byte-count / 8; all fit imm <= 0x7FFFFFFF. Leaf function.
  //
  // Called from every ttk_case_* before invoking tokenize so a stale
  // token-output buffer from a previous case cannot false-positive a
  // per-token assert.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let ttk_reset : () -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R106.SHELL-003 (paideia-os/shell#42): zero every fixture .bss slot then populate each input buffer with the case byte sequence via mov_b constants. Same pattern tlx_reset establishes at tests/test_lexer.pdx (byte-write over rodata string literals so buffers containing 0x22, 0x27, 0x5C are unambiguous at read time). Zeros the 32-qword _ttk_tokens_out first so a case's per-token assert cannot see a stale record from a prior case. r10 = .bss LEA scratch, rax = byte constant / zero. Leaf function; no push/pop parity concern. Labels prefixed ttk_rs_ (none needed -- linear body).",
      block: {
        // Zero every fixture buffer.
        xor rax, rax;

        // _ttk_in_empty (1 qword).
        lea r10, [rip + _ttk_in_empty];
        mov [r10 + 0], rax;

        // _ttk_in_ws (1 qword).
        lea r10, [rip + _ttk_in_ws];
        mov [r10 + 0], rax;

        // _ttk_in_bare (1 qword).
        lea r10, [rip + _ttk_in_bare];
        mov [r10 + 0], rax;

        // _ttk_in_two (1 qword).
        lea r10, [rip + _ttk_in_two];
        mov [r10 + 0], rax;

        // _ttk_in_sq (2 qwords).
        lea r10, [rip + _ttk_in_sq];
        mov [r10 + 0], rax;
        mov [r10 + 8], rax;

        // _ttk_in_dq (2 qwords).
        lea r10, [rip + _ttk_in_dq];
        mov [r10 + 0], rax;
        mov [r10 + 8], rax;

        // _ttk_in_dq_esc (1 qword).
        lea r10, [rip + _ttk_in_dq_esc];
        mov [r10 + 0], rax;

        // _ttk_in_bare_esc (1 qword).
        lea r10, [rip + _ttk_in_bare_esc];
        mov [r10 + 0], rax;

        // _ttk_in_adj (2 qwords).
        lea r10, [rip + _ttk_in_adj];
        mov [r10 + 0], rax;
        mov [r10 + 8], rax;

        // _ttk_in_unterm_sq (1 qword).
        lea r10, [rip + _ttk_in_unterm_sq];
        mov [r10 + 0], rax;

        // _ttk_in_unterm_dq (1 qword).
        lea r10, [rip + _ttk_in_unterm_dq];
        mov [r10 + 0], rax;

        // _ttk_in_inv_esc (1 qword).
        lea r10, [rip + _ttk_in_inv_esc];
        mov [r10 + 0], rax;

        // _ttk_in_two_of (1 qword).
        lea r10, [rip + _ttk_in_two_of];
        mov [r10 + 0], rax;

        // _ttk_tokens_out (32 qwords).
        lea r10, [rip + _ttk_tokens_out];
        mov [r10 +   0], rax;
        mov [r10 +   8], rax;
        mov [r10 +  16], rax;
        mov [r10 +  24], rax;
        mov [r10 +  32], rax;
        mov [r10 +  40], rax;
        mov [r10 +  48], rax;
        mov [r10 +  56], rax;
        mov [r10 +  64], rax;
        mov [r10 +  72], rax;
        mov [r10 +  80], rax;
        mov [r10 +  88], rax;
        mov [r10 +  96], rax;
        mov [r10 + 104], rax;
        mov [r10 + 112], rax;
        mov [r10 + 120], rax;
        mov [r10 + 128], rax;
        mov [r10 + 136], rax;
        mov [r10 + 144], rax;
        mov [r10 + 152], rax;
        mov [r10 + 160], rax;
        mov [r10 + 168], rax;
        mov [r10 + 176], rax;
        mov [r10 + 184], rax;
        mov [r10 + 192], rax;
        mov [r10 + 200], rax;
        mov [r10 + 208], rax;
        mov [r10 + 216], rax;
        mov [r10 + 224], rax;
        mov [r10 + 232], rax;
        mov [r10 + 240], rax;
        mov [r10 + 248], rax;

        // Populate _ttk_in_ws: "   " (3 spaces).
        lea r10, [rip + _ttk_in_ws];
        mov rax, 0x20; mov_b [r10 + 0], rax;
        mov rax, 0x20; mov_b [r10 + 1], rax;
        mov rax, 0x20; mov_b [r10 + 2], rax;

        // Populate _ttk_in_bare: "ls" (2 bytes).
        lea r10, [rip + _ttk_in_bare];
        mov rax, 0x6c; mov_b [r10 + 0], rax;    // 'l'
        mov rax, 0x73; mov_b [r10 + 1], rax;    // 's'

        // Populate _ttk_in_two: "ls -l" (5 bytes).
        lea r10, [rip + _ttk_in_two];
        mov rax, 0x6c; mov_b [r10 + 0], rax;    // 'l'
        mov rax, 0x73; mov_b [r10 + 1], rax;    // 's'
        mov rax, 0x20; mov_b [r10 + 2], rax;    // ' '
        mov rax, 0x2d; mov_b [r10 + 3], rax;    // '-'
        mov rax, 0x6c; mov_b [r10 + 4], rax;    // 'l'

        // Populate _ttk_in_sq: "echo 'a b'" (10 bytes).
        lea r10, [rip + _ttk_in_sq];
        mov rax, 0x65; mov_b [r10 + 0], rax;    // 'e'
        mov rax, 0x63; mov_b [r10 + 1], rax;    // 'c'
        mov rax, 0x68; mov_b [r10 + 2], rax;    // 'h'
        mov rax, 0x6f; mov_b [r10 + 3], rax;    // 'o'
        mov rax, 0x20; mov_b [r10 + 4], rax;    // ' '
        mov rax, 0x27; mov_b [r10 + 5], rax;    // '\''
        mov rax, 0x61; mov_b [r10 + 6], rax;    // 'a'
        mov rax, 0x20; mov_b [r10 + 7], rax;    // ' '
        mov rax, 0x62; mov_b [r10 + 8], rax;    // 'b'
        mov rax, 0x27; mov_b [r10 + 9], rax;    // '\''

        // Populate _ttk_in_dq: 'echo "a b"' (10 bytes).
        lea r10, [rip + _ttk_in_dq];
        mov rax, 0x65; mov_b [r10 + 0], rax;    // 'e'
        mov rax, 0x63; mov_b [r10 + 1], rax;    // 'c'
        mov rax, 0x68; mov_b [r10 + 2], rax;    // 'h'
        mov rax, 0x6f; mov_b [r10 + 3], rax;    // 'o'
        mov rax, 0x20; mov_b [r10 + 4], rax;    // ' '
        mov rax, 0x22; mov_b [r10 + 5], rax;    // '"'
        mov rax, 0x61; mov_b [r10 + 6], rax;    // 'a'
        mov rax, 0x20; mov_b [r10 + 7], rax;    // ' '
        mov rax, 0x62; mov_b [r10 + 8], rax;    // 'b'
        mov rax, 0x22; mov_b [r10 + 9], rax;    // '"'

        // Populate _ttk_in_dq_esc: '"a\b"' (5 bytes).
        // Bytes: 0x22 'a' 0x5C 'b' 0x22. The '\' inside "..." consumes
        // the next byte ('b') as literal, then the trailing '"' closes.
        // Expected: 1 token spanning (0, 5).
        lea r10, [rip + _ttk_in_dq_esc];
        mov rax, 0x22; mov_b [r10 + 0], rax;    // '"'
        mov rax, 0x61; mov_b [r10 + 1], rax;    // 'a'
        mov rax, 0x5c; mov_b [r10 + 2], rax;    // '\\'
        mov rax, 0x62; mov_b [r10 + 3], rax;    // 'b'
        mov rax, 0x22; mov_b [r10 + 4], rax;    // '"'

        // Populate _ttk_in_bare_esc: "a\b" (3 bytes).
        // Bytes: 'a' 0x5C 'b'. Bare '\' outside quotes consumes itself
        // + next byte as literal, so the word body is 3 bytes long and
        // no whitespace intervenes -- one token (0, 3).
        lea r10, [rip + _ttk_in_bare_esc];
        mov rax, 0x61; mov_b [r10 + 0], rax;    // 'a'
        mov rax, 0x5c; mov_b [r10 + 1], rax;    // '\\'
        mov rax, 0x62; mov_b [r10 + 2], rax;    // 'b'

        // Populate _ttk_in_adj: "hello'a b'world" (15 bytes).
        // The single-quote fragment sits BETWEEN bare word fragments,
        // so all three glue into ONE token spanning (0, 15). This is
        // the adjacent-fragment invariant the tokenizer header calls
        // out (src/tokenizer.pdx §"SIGNATURE").
        lea r10, [rip + _ttk_in_adj];
        mov rax, 0x68; mov_b [r10 +  0], rax;   // 'h'
        mov rax, 0x65; mov_b [r10 +  1], rax;   // 'e'
        mov rax, 0x6c; mov_b [r10 +  2], rax;   // 'l'
        mov rax, 0x6c; mov_b [r10 +  3], rax;   // 'l'
        mov rax, 0x6f; mov_b [r10 +  4], rax;   // 'o'
        mov rax, 0x27; mov_b [r10 +  5], rax;   // '\''
        mov rax, 0x61; mov_b [r10 +  6], rax;   // 'a'
        mov rax, 0x20; mov_b [r10 +  7], rax;   // ' '
        mov rax, 0x62; mov_b [r10 +  8], rax;   // 'b'
        mov rax, 0x27; mov_b [r10 +  9], rax;   // '\''
        mov rax, 0x77; mov_b [r10 + 10], rax;   // 'w'
        mov rax, 0x6f; mov_b [r10 + 11], rax;   // 'o'
        mov rax, 0x72; mov_b [r10 + 12], rax;   // 'r'
        mov rax, 0x6c; mov_b [r10 + 13], rax;   // 'l'
        mov rax, 0x64; mov_b [r10 + 14], rax;   // 'd'

        // Populate _ttk_in_unterm_sq: "'ab" (3 bytes).
        // Opening 0x27, then 'a' 'b', no closing 0x27. Expected:
        // TOK_ERR_UNTERMINATED_QUOTE (0xFFFFEB02).
        lea r10, [rip + _ttk_in_unterm_sq];
        mov rax, 0x27; mov_b [r10 + 0], rax;    // '\''
        mov rax, 0x61; mov_b [r10 + 1], rax;    // 'a'
        mov rax, 0x62; mov_b [r10 + 2], rax;    // 'b'

        // Populate _ttk_in_unterm_dq: '"ab' (3 bytes).
        // Opening 0x22, then 'a' 'b', no closing 0x22. Expected:
        // TOK_ERR_UNTERMINATED_QUOTE (0xFFFFEB02).
        lea r10, [rip + _ttk_in_unterm_dq];
        mov rax, 0x22; mov_b [r10 + 0], rax;    // '"'
        mov rax, 0x61; mov_b [r10 + 1], rax;    // 'a'
        mov rax, 0x62; mov_b [r10 + 2], rax;    // 'b'

        // Populate _ttk_in_inv_esc: "a\" (2 bytes).
        // 'a' then bare 0x5C at EOL. tk_bare_escape consumes '\' then
        // tries to consume the next byte and finds EOL -> INVALID_ESCAPE
        // (0xFFFFEB03). Distinct from UNTERMINATED_QUOTE because no
        // opening quote was ever entered.
        lea r10, [rip + _ttk_in_inv_esc];
        mov rax, 0x61; mov_b [r10 + 0], rax;    // 'a'
        mov rax, 0x5c; mov_b [r10 + 1], rax;    // '\\'

        // Populate _ttk_in_two_of: "a b" (3 bytes).
        // Two words separated by whitespace. With cap=1 passed at the
        // call site, the SECOND word emit trips the OVERFLOW gate at
        // tk_outer's `cmp rbp, r15; jae tk_overflow`. Expected:
        // 0xFFFFEB04 with _ttk_tokens_out[0] left holding the first
        // record (offset=0, length=1) from the earlier emit and every
        // later slot untouched (ttk_reset zeroed them).
        lea r10, [rip + _ttk_in_two_of];
        mov rax, 0x61; mov_b [r10 + 0], rax;    // 'a'
        mov rax, 0x20; mov_b [r10 + 1], rax;    // ' '
        mov rax, 0x62; mov_b [r10 + 2], rax;    // 'b'

        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // Per-case helpers: each case pushes rbx, r12, sub rsp 8 (24-byte
  // prologue: rsp%16==8 on entry, ==0 after prologue). The nested
  // `tokenize` call sees rsp%16==0. The matched pop order at every
  // return: add rsp, 8; pop r12; pop rbx; ret.
  //
  // Register usage inside a case (after the prologue):
  //   rax           tokenize return, then per-assert scratch
  //   rdi/rsi/rdx/rcx  tokenize args (loaded fresh per call)
  //   r10           .bss LEA scratch
  //   r12           _ttk_tokens_out base (loaded once, reused per assert)
  //   rbx           spare callee-save (unused today; reserved for a
  //                 future loop-driven case that iterates per token)
  // ==========================================================================

  // ==========================================================================
  // ttk_case_empty() -> u64
  //
  // Input: len=0 (line_ptr = _ttk_in_empty, any valid buffer). Expected:
  // rax == 0 (zero tokens), output buffer untouched. Exercises the
  // tk_done_empty short-circuit in the SUT -- no dereferencing of
  // line_ptr, no token emit, no output writes.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let ttk_case_empty : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R106.SHELL-003 (paideia-os/shell#42) case empty: tokenize called with line_len=0 returns rax=0 (zero tokens) via the tk_done_empty short-circuit at src/tokenizer.pdx:178. No dereference of line_ptr, no writes to tokens_out. Assert token count == 0 only; a per-token assert would be vacuous. Prologue push rbx + push r12 + sub rsp,8 (rsp%16==0 for the nested call).",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        sub rsp, 8;

        call ttk_reset;

        lea rdi, [rip + _ttk_in_empty];
        mov rsi, 0;
        lea rdx, [rip + _ttk_tokens_out];
        mov rcx, 16;                              // TTK_TOKENS_CAP
        call tokenize;

        cmp rax, 0;
        jne ttk_empty_fail;

        xor rax, rax;                             // TTK_PASS
        add rsp, 8;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret;

      ttk_empty_fail:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFEDB0;                      // TTK_FAIL_EMPTY
        add rsp, 8;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

